[Music]  instead of doing a video based on zelda  lore i want to go back to my roots for a  change  this channel saw its inception as a  place where i discuss gameplay elements  and  in particular dungeon mechanics and  that's what we're gonna be doing today  the idea for this video was inspired by  a modder by the name of waikutero  who not too long ago premiered the first  large-scale custom dungeon for breath of  the wild  now i'm not supposed to promote mods but  it exists  and i do think it's fair and harmless to  use it as a case study  i've already talked about the divine  beasts and shrines extensively  so i'm not going to repeat myself again  and will instead refer you to my very  first video which covers all my praises  complaints and suggestions about them  what i want to do here is take a look at  some specific dungeon mechanics  and see which ones i think should go  which ones are worth bringing back  and potentially expanded or altered for  the highly anticipated sequel to breath  of the wild  some things i won't be covering are  enemies and bosses specific dungeon  items and aesthetics i want to purely  focus on aspects like puzzle mechanics  tools for navigation quality of life  improvements and other topics revolving  around progression  challenge or traversal so let's dive  right in  let's start off with a simple quality of  life improvement reducing scripted  camera events  if there's one thing i really dislike  about revisiting the older 3d games  it is the constant need to freeze you in  your tracks and show you what you've  just accomplished or give you hints  about the room you just entered  even if it's something you've already  done many times before  opening a door changing water currents  or water levels  manipulating any other aspects of the  dungeon heck even showing you the same  unnecessary cutscene for switches you've  been pulling or hitting since the very  beginning of the game  we get it we hit the ice switch and it  closes we also don't need to see a door  opening every time we defeat all enemies  or solve a puzzle in a room  taking control away from the player like  this constantly might have felt  necessary in the old days when we were  still getting used to navigating a 3d  space  but nowadays it's just wasting your time  i get that in some cases when you do  something significant it looks cool and  cinematic to show parts of the dungeon  changing but this should only be  reserved for big events and only the  first time you trigger it  breath of the wild already handled this  concept way better when you manipulate  the divine beast from the map screen it  doesn't show you a cutscene of the  dungeon moving  it just happens and you can observe the  effects while still having freedom of  movement  triggering switches doesn't always show  you exactly what changed in the room  it's up to you to look around and  observe what the effects are  but the game does have the tendency to  fall into the old habits at times too  for instance unless the trigger to a  door is right in front of it and  impossible to miss  it still feels the need to show you the  door opening it still needs to play a  cutscene each time a chest  opens after clearing up a cobbling camp  something which should become obvious  after the first few times sometimes it  shows you something changing in a room  sometimes it doesn't it isn't always  consistent at handling it  it's already a huge step up from the old  games but i think it could be taken  further there are plenty of alternatives  to provide the player with information  without stopping them in their tracks  we already have the classic fanfare  which plays whenever you do something  significant  and in a lot of cases the sound of a  door opening should already suffice to  communicate to the player that they  succeeded  especially if the room you're in only  has one way in and out  on top of that you could do something  other games have done in the past and  show the cut scene of the door opening  in a small separate window in the corner  of the screen  or if you have access to a mini-map just  show something like a blinking icon  indicating where a door has opened  the freedom found in breath of the wild  is one of its greatest strengths  and is one of the aspects which got the  most praised by players and critics  alike  but in my opinion there is such a thing  as too much freedom  at least in terms of dungeons now for  the overworld the freedom worked and was  a nice change of pace for the series  which in the past  barred off large sections of the  overworld because you weren't supposed  to be there yet  this felt like hand-holding and after so  many games started to become very  formalic  but for dungeons this is a different  story a lot of people use hyrule castle  as a prime example  of how dungeons could or should have  been and while i think it's an amazing  place to explore  i would be hard-pressed to point to it  as a poster child for the sequel the  reason being that it's simply too  open-ended to function as a proper  dungeon reaching the boss should be  an ordeal a difficult but satisfying  road to success  something that's simply missing from the  castle since you can skip almost all of  it if you feel like it  placing restrictions on the player and  forcing them through a trial of rooms  puzzles and enemies  isn't always a bad thing that isn't to  say that there shouldn't be optional  sections  i'm all for rewarding the player for  some additional exploration that isn't  mandatory  but having 95 of the whole layout be  completely optional  is a bit too much for my taste i would  definitely want to find some middle  ground here  this also ties into the idea of having  all the tools needed to tackle each  dungeon from the very get-go i don't  know about you but i actually like  stumbling upon a dungeon which i'm  unable to beat or even enter  right away one that requires an item or  key which is found in a different  dungeon or maybe obtained through a  seemingly unrelated side quest somewhere  in the overworld  you will make a mental note of it and  continue on with your adventure but you  will always have that location in the  back of your mind  and you can't wait for the day that you  finally find what you need to enter and  look forward to seeing what's inside  what challenges await you and the cool  stuff you might find in there  dungeons can still be tackled out of  order but some might require  overcoming a different challenge first  not every dungeon needs to be accessible  right away  the best example of this is found in the  original on the nes  a game which breath of the wild took the  most inspiration from  the first three dungeons can be entered  and even completed out of order but  there are also some later in the game  which require finding a specific item  first  freedom is nice but some restrictions  should also be in place to create this  anticipation  something to mark on your map and keep  in the back of your mind  speaking of maps let's talk about the  map historically maps in video games  have either been  close to useless or absolutely crucial  to keep track of where you've been  in the zelda series this kinda differs  from game to game  in some installments i barely even look  at the map and finding it gets nothing  more than a  meh out of me other times like in the  example of skull woods or ganon's tower  in a link to the past  the dungeon is so convoluted and  disconnected with a huge part of it  being completely optional that finding  the map is actually very convenient  i think breath of the wild took it a  step too far turning the map into a  glorified gps  being able to see exactly where you are  in real time is convenient  sure but it also kinda takes away from  the mystery if you already know exactly  what lies beyond a certain room or  corridor  this goes especially for hyrule castle  as somehow we have a map of the entire  structure from the moment of entering we  never even had to acquire it and in the  divine beast getting the map is the  first thing you are forced to do  since without it you cannot manipulate  the dungeon and thus you are unable to  access  most of it and progressing through the  dungeon just becomes a follow the dots  at that point  i would personally want to revert back  to the old map style but at the same  time i would also make some changes to  it  i do think it's nice that the map  highlights which room you are currently  in like in the old games but seeing your  exact position within each individual  room  is a bit much the same thing goes for  being able to see every treasure chest  on the map  after obtaining the compass which also  kinda takes away from the mystery  in fact i would go as far as to say that  the compass can be excluded altogether  something breath of the wild already did  away with however treasure chests inside  the dungeons were handled  quite poorly this had everything to do  with the fact that after clearing a  divine beast you are no longer allowed  to re-enter it and because of this none  of the chests hold anything substantial  no unique apparel weapons or upgrades at  least nothing you wouldn't eventually be  able to find or buy in the overworld  since not obtaining all chests inside  the beast during your first playthrough  would mean you would never be able to  100  the game that being said it would be  nice if you could somehow keep track of  which doors are locked but rather than  showing this from the very beginning i  would go the silent hill route and only  show that a door is locked after you've  already encountered it we could even go  a step further  instead of link automatically making  notes of this on the map  this responsibility could be handed to  the player in the past we've seen this  in phantom hourglass and spirit tracks  where you can actually draw on your map  now obviously without a touch screen and  a stylus this would be very inconvenient  so instead i would just use the stamp  mechanic from breath of the wild  overworld map  i mean why fix what isn't broken the  player could mark locations of chests  keys locked doors or any other important  findings themselves  and the best thing about it is that it's  completely optional if you have a good  memory or don't feel like fiddling  around with the map  you don't have to i also think that the  map would be given more meaning if it's  something well hidden and isn't always  handed out early on  finding it should be something  significant after wandering around  without a clue of what the dungeons  layout actually looks like  you finally have something to hold onto  something to help you orient yourself  this should never be a given which leads  into the next subject  if there's one thing that the 3d  installments are guilty of when it comes  to dungeons  is that they tend to fall into the same  habit of giving you everything in a set  order  making it all feel very formulaic i  actually forgot to mention this in my  video about the beauty of 2d zelda games  for that video specifically i decided to  revisit the nes original  oracle seasons and a link to the past  and one of the first things i was  reminded of was the variation in its  progression  sometimes you find the main item very  early on other times  close to the end sometimes you find two  items or the item you find isn't needed  to beat the dungeon  in some rare cases the item needed isn't  even found inside the dungeon but in the  overworld somewhere and the boss door  doesn't always lead straight to the boss  but rather  unlocks a whole new section breath of  the wild too could definitely learn from  this and might even be able to take it a  step further  not every dungeon needs a main item or  even a map  going back to silent hill for a moment  some of the maps or better weapons in  the quote-unquote dungeon sections of  the game are so well hidden that some  players never even find them and simply  soldier through without them  some areas purposely don't have a map  where the protagonist draws his own as  he ventures from room to room if a place  is supposed to be  a maze handing the player a map kinda  defeats the purpose  i'm sure you all have your own examples  and experiences with games which handle  things differently  and how it sparks the mystery and  satisfaction of finding out on your own  where you are and what you need to  venture further  it is at that point that a dungeon stops  feeling like a video game level with  predetermined rules and more like an  actual place you are exploring the  feeling of being the first in a long  time to set foot within its walls and  discover its secrets and history  lastly i will touch upon the aspect of  survival some consider breath of the  wild's overworld to be the biggest  dungeon in zelda history while i  wouldn't exactly call it as such myself  i do think dungeons could learn from  breath of the wild's overworld where you  don't just smash your way through them  a dungeon should feel like a  self-contained adventure in and of  itself  a dangerous place where you might be  stuck for quite some time and you need  to make do with what you brought in with  you  or find inside this presents the idea of  dungeons having certain safe spots  places to rest cook food or catch your  breath for a while  similar to the rooms found in the trial  of the sword a safe space where there's  no enemies and you can just hang out for  a bit while you plan your next route or  move  these rooms could also double as a place  to save your game  generally in breath of the wild you can  save your game anytime and anywhere you  want but there are some exceptions like  event island and the aforementioned  trial of the sword where the game  restricts you from saving mid challenge  of course i wouldn't go as far as not  being able to save at all while being  inside of a dungeon hence why safe spots  to do this  could be a nice option it's never been  done in a zelda game before  but it has in games like dark souls  where the bonfires act as save and spawn  points for the player  there's something about overcoming a  dangerous challenge and breathing a sigh  of relief when you finally find a place  to rest and save your progress  it also makes a game over a lot more  significant if you haven't found a safe  spot in a while  making the dungeon more challenging in  the process and while we're on the topic  of challenge and survival  let's talk about healing though this  isn't necessarily a mechanic unique to  dungeons alone i do think it has a  pretty big impact on it  it's a complaint that many have already  pointed out when the game was still  brand new  and that is that being able to heal  yourself from within the menu  is just too overpowered i think this  mechanic definitely deserves an overhaul  in breath of the wild too  at first breath of the wild seemed  intimidating because enemies had a much  higher damage output than in previous  titles  and on top of that hearts were  completely eliminated from the game  but in reality the food mechanic means  you are carrying a huge collection of  hearts with you at all times  there should definitely be a much bigger  restriction on it some of these  restrictions could include limiting the  amount of food you can devour in a  certain time span  making your hearts restore gradually  upon eating instead of filling them all  up instantaneously  or perhaps most effectively just go to  dark souls route and turn the healing  process into something real time and not  something you can just do from the menu  screen  one or more of these measures should do  the trick and i cannot wait to see link  desperately running from a lino while  trying to stuff a roasted chicken  into his mouth and with that i will  conclude this video  as you can see from the points i've  covered some dungeon mechanics were  already improved in breath of the wild  but if the sequel were to go back to the  more traditional dungeon style  there's definitely something to be  learned from the old but also from the  new approach  breath of the wild's open-ended nature  might have been a step too far when it  came to dungeons but the same thing goes  for the more formulaic and restrictive  construction found in the older games  as mentioned before a middle ground  would be the key i want to thank  waikuteru for giving me permission to  use some of his footage  if you want to see more regarding the  subject of dungeons i've made a couple  of videos on the topic of progression  items  dungeon traversal preparation aesthetics  and atmosphere  etc which can be found in the  description as well as links to  waikatero's videos on his channel  as always a big thanks to my amazing  patreons channel members and the folks  on my discord who all work together to  make it a lively place to be  additionally a quick shout out to merlin  cooads for upgrading their pledge on  patreon  and to coocolover for doing an amazing  job arranging all the new discord  functionality  thank you all so much for the help and  support i will see you all in the next  video this is dawn signing off and  have a good one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

